  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Firms, chambers and clients pay for accuracy and clear writing. They judge you by drafts, notes and reliability.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is a plain-language redraft of one clause.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and free courses for law students: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools draft clauses and summarise judgments, and they can get citations wrong. Routine drafting shrinks first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Verification, judgment and accountability hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI for first drafts and check every citation; later, own the advice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general free courses for law students path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Compliance route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Steady demand</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Less courtroom work</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Legal tech route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Growing area</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs tech skill</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Judicial services route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Public service path</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Long preparation</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn free courses for law students cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Free courses for law students is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM law-related lessons.</li>
    <li>Free public judgments and bare acts.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube lessons.</li>
    <li>A lawyer who reviews your drafts.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In three weeks, redraft three clauses in plain language and ask a lawyer to mark each one.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skills">Four skills and how to practise each for free</h2>

  <p>
    Choose skills your target roles ask for. Litigation, corporate and policy paths lean on different mixes.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Skill</th>
          <th>Free way to practise</th>
          <th>Proof to keep</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Skill"><strong>Legal drafting</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Free way to practise">Redraft a simple clause in plain language</td>
          <td data-label="Proof to keep">Before and after samples</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Skill"><strong>Research</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Free way to practise">Summarise one public judgment in a page</td>
          <td data-label="Proof to keep">A research note with sources</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Skill"><strong>Contract reading</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Free way to practise">Read a public sample agreement and list risks</td>
          <td data-label="Proof to keep">A short risk list</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Skill"><strong>Communication</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Free way to practise">Explain one rule aloud in two minutes</td>
          <td data-label="Proof to keep">A recording or script</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Ask a lawyer or senior to mark the weakest line in each sample.
  </p>

  <h2 id="caution">What to check before paying for any legal course</h2>

  <p>
    Some paid courses add value, but many topics have free equivalents.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Check</th>
          <th>Good sign</th>
          <th>Warning sign</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Check"><strong>Who teaches</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good sign">Practising lawyers with a public record</td>
          <td data-label="Warning sign">No names</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Check"><strong>Output</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good sign">Drafts you can review</td>
          <td data-label="Warning sign">Certificate only</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Check"><strong>Fee</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good sign">Clear price and refund terms</td>
          <td data-label="Warning sign">Pressure to join today</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Check"><strong>Claims</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good sign">Honest about outcomes</td>
          <td data-label="Warning sign">Guaranteed placements</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money: course fees and the 10 percent test</h2>

  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree or course fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution that needs course-specific evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 8 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 80,000 for the main course, and a fifth is Rs 1.6 lakh as a serious caution. The rest can fund a laptop, practice time and a reserve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test the field with free courses and one finished project before paying for a long programme. Do not borrow heavily for a course whose graduates cannot show placement in a specific role.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for this career</h2>

  <p>
    Test the choice with <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>: Biology, Context, Market and Survival.
  </p>
  <div class="protocol">
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Biology: do you like careful reading and writing?</div>
        <p>Redraft one clause and notice.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Context: time, money and home support</div>
        <p>Count time and support.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Market: who hires?</div>
        <p>Read ten notices for roles you want.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Survival: what survives AI?</div>
        <p>Drafting shrinks. Judgment holds value.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

